Cortex-M4 RPMsg ping-pong firmware for the i.MX RT1170
=======================================================

rpmsg_lite_pingpong_rtos_cm4/ is the CM4 half of the rpmsg_lite_pingpong_rtos
example of the NXP MCUXpresso SDK 2.16.000 for the MIMXRT1170-EVK, as the SDK
exports it as a standalone armgcc project. It is changed to run under Linux
remoteproc:
- no MCMGR;
- a resource table, in the .resource_table section;
- the vrings at 0x20236000, the end of OCRAM M4, which the rootfs device tree
  reserves;
- notifications over MU channel 1;
- no debug console, and no pin muxing, so starting it changes no pad Linux
  uses.

For a debug console on LPUART7 (GPIO_AD_00/01), set DEBUG_CONSOLE_UART_INDEX
to 7 in rpmsg_lite_pingpong_rtos_cm4/board/board.h. On the i.MX RT1170 EVK
those pads are the touch controller's interrupt and reset, so touch stops
working once the CM4 is started, until the board is reset. On the
MaaXBoard-RT, what those pads connect to is not verified.

Linux loads it from /lib/firmware/rpmsg_lite_pingpong_rtos_cm4.elf, and the
imx_rpmsg_pingpong module talks to it:

  insmod /lib/modules/imx_rpmsg_pingpong.ko
  echo -n rpmsg_lite_pingpong_rtos_cm4.elf > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/firmware
  echo start > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state

The CM4 cannot be stopped once started; reset the board to start it again.

Building
--------

The rootfs project builds it for IMXRT117X_NXPEVK and MAAXBOARD_RT. "make" in
this directory builds it alone. Both need cmake and the arm-none-eabi GCC
toolchain with newlib, which the BSP's Docker build image provides. ARMGCC_DIR
names the toolchain's prefix (default /usr).

Licences
--------

As the SDK's SW-Content-Register gives them:

- CMSIS/: Apache-2.0, in LICENSE-Apache-2.0.txt (from CMSIS 5.9.0);
- the FreeRTOS kernel files under freertos/, and source/FreeRTOSConfig.h:
  MIT, with the text in each file;
- every other file, including NXP's own files under freertos/ (the CMake
  files and freertos_tasks_c_additions.h): BSD-3-Clause, in COPYING-BSD-3.
